WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.160
Overdoses, relapses, and rehab doesn't&nbsp;
sound like a recipe for a successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.720
comedy - but Chuck Lorre pulled it off for&nbsp;
eight seasons with his smash hit "Mom."

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:16.080
If you've ever dreamed of making it in showbiz and&nbsp;
need a bit of inspiration, check out Chuck Lorre's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:21.240
story. Lorre, the creator of "Mom," along with a&nbsp;
host of other hit sitcoms, spent many years broke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:26.160
and struggling before finally breaking through in&nbsp;
his mid-30s. As he told the Lansing State Journal:

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.040
"I remember vividly what it's like to put 38 cents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.640
in the gas tank and drive to my second&nbsp;
cousin's house, so they would feed me."

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:37.080
Before hitting it big in television, however,&nbsp;
Lorre spent some time dabbling in another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.920
creative profession – songwriting. And if&nbsp;
you're wondering whether any of his songs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:45.360
became well-known, we've got four words&nbsp;
for you: "Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles."

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.440
Yes, if you've ever gotten the "Teenage Mutant&nbsp;
Ninja Turtles" theme song stuck in your head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.880
you can thank Lorre, who co-wrote the catchy&nbsp;
song that helped define the franchise. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:57.000
for his comedy-writing dream, that&nbsp;
didn't really happen until he got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.380
a job writing for the groundbreaking sitcom&nbsp;
"Roseanne," a major break that opened doors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.900
launching him on a career&nbsp;
trajectory like no other.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.700
After Roseanne, Lorre went on to become one of&nbsp;
the most successful sitcom creators in history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:13.200
responsible for the hits "Grace Under&nbsp;
Fire," "Cybill," and "Dharma and Greg."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.520
But Lorre really hit the jackpot with "Two&nbsp;
and a Half Men" and "The Big Bang Theory,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.840
the pair of megahits which gave him the&nbsp;
Hollywood cred to make "Mom" happen. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.160
even though Lorre is a rarity – a sitcom&nbsp;
svengali with multiple mega-hits under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.540
his belt – any aspiring writer can find&nbsp;
inspiration in his rags-to-riches tale.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.180
Before "Mom," Anna Faris was best known for&nbsp;
her roles in comedies like "Just Friends,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:40.140
"The House Bunny," and the "Scary Movie"&nbsp;
franchise. With her distinctive comedic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:44.340
chops and bubbly blonde persona, she was&nbsp;
the first actor CBS approached for "Mom."

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.800
Faris told PopSugar that her husband at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.980
actor Chris Pratt, actually read&nbsp;
the script for "Mom" before she did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.660
and by the time he got to the second page he was&nbsp;
convinced the role was perfect for her, saying:

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.480
"Honey, whatever you're doing,&nbsp;
stop now and read this."

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:02.040
Even though television has come a long way&nbsp;
since the days where everything edgy was on HBO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:06.120
Faris and Janney agreed that "Mom"&nbsp;
dealt with risky issues for a network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.420
sitcom during a joint interview&nbsp;
with Larry King. When King asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.960
Faris if she was ever surprised by the&nbsp;
content of the show, she revealed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.680
she was constantly shocked by how far the&nbsp;
writers were willing to push it, saying:

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:21.900
"On a weekly basis when we get the new scripts I&nbsp;
always say, 'Are we sure CBS is okay with this?'"

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.620
Before "Mom," Allison Janney was best known for

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:30.900
her role as White House press secretary Claudia&nbsp;
Jean Cregg, also known as CJ, on "The West Wing."

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.320
Fans may be surprised to learn that&nbsp;
she didn't have her breakthrough role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.680
on the "West Wing," until she was 40,&nbsp;
which is pretty unusual by Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.720
standards. Janney did have a steady stream&nbsp;
of supporting movie and TV roles before then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.140
but nothing as big as "The West&nbsp;
Wing," which eventually led to "Mom."

00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:49.140
On "Mom," Allison Janney's character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:54.060
Bonnie, had long, flowing auburn hair. Her coif&nbsp;
actually became synonymous with the character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:58.140
which was a bit ironic considering that&nbsp;
it's not even Janney's real hair. Yup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:01.740
the actor was wearing a wig the whole time&nbsp;
— and her co-workers didn't even know!

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.280
Janney's real hair is actually a&nbsp;
beautiful shade of natural silver,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:09.240
and when she showed up on set without the wig&nbsp;
one day, the producers apparently freaked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.960
During an appearance on "The Tonight Show&nbsp;
Starring Jimmy Fallon," Janney told the tale.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:18.660
“'What are we gonna do, you should've asked&nbsp;
us before changing your hair!' I'm like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:22.368
guys, I've been wearing a wig since&nbsp;
season one, but nobody knew it!"

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:27.240
As a recovering addict himself, "Mom"&nbsp;
was a very personal show for Chuck Lorre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.900
who got sober himself at the&nbsp;
age of 47. As he told TV Guide:

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:35.820
"Sobriety is a big part of my life, and&nbsp;
it's been that way for almost 13 years…I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.340
eternally grateful I've been&nbsp;
able to find this in my life."

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:43.140
"I am trying so hard. I quit&nbsp;
drinking, I'm going to meetings…"

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:48.180
As far as how his personal recovery journey&nbsp;
relates to "Mom," Lorre told IndieWire:

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.280
"Broken people trying to repair their lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.880
I can relate to that. I think we're all&nbsp;
in the process of doing a little mending."

00:03:53.880 --> 00:04:00.540
"Excuse me, no one's going to be&nbsp;
snorting any fat rails while I'm here."

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.000
And, as Janney told IndieWire&nbsp;
in another interview:

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.380
"It feels like a very grown-up show for&nbsp;
[Chuck Lorre]. I had no idea that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.020
were going to be going to the places&nbsp;
that we've gotten to go with 'Mom.'"

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:16.200
In showbusiness, like any other business, success&nbsp;
breeds success. And in Chuck Lorre’s case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:20.760
having so many hits on his resume gave him&nbsp;
the freedom to take on riskier stuff — in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.180
other words, shows like "Two and a Half&nbsp;
Men" gave him the freedom to make "Mom."

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:28.020
Lorre told IndieWire that to challenge&nbsp;
himself creatively, he needed to tackle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:31.980
something different, something that would&nbsp;
force him to step into new areas of comedy.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:36.360
"I am at a point in my career where I can take&nbsp;
some chances...If you're going to experiment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:40.140
do something that is meaningful to you. If you&nbsp;
fail, you did something meaningful to you."

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:42.360
And with "Mom," he did just that.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:44.280
"Someone's spoiling themselves."

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:49.740
"It's for my mom. She's a drug&nbsp;
addict who just got out of jail."

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:52.200
"aw!"

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:58.200
Back in the 1970s, legendary TV producer Norman&nbsp;
Lear was a household name. He had many creations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:01.980
to his name, but none as groundbreaking&nbsp;
as "All in the Family," which dealt with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:06.240
topical issues and proved that TV sitcoms&nbsp;
could be intelligent and thought provoking.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:11.340
Chuck Lorre often credits Lear for inspiring&nbsp;
him to create similarly edgy sitcoms – proving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:15.060
to the world that television doesn’t have&nbsp;
to be predictable, and it doesn’t need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:18.660
be insulting to the intelligence of the&nbsp;
audience. In an interview with Variety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:21.900
Lorre said that with "Mom," he wanted&nbsp;
to tell stories that could take place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:26.940
in what he calls a "Norman Lear world," in&nbsp;
other words – realism, rather than fantasy.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:29.160
"You know the definition of insanity…"

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.560
"Yes I know the definition&nbsp;
of insanity I live with you!"

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.040
Lear, now 100 years old and&nbsp;
someone Lorre considers a friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.520
brought a reality to the sitcom&nbsp;
format that had never been seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:42.480
before. And much like Lorre has done with&nbsp;
"Mom," Lear made history tackling tough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:46.560
subjects on TV as well. In an interview&nbsp;
with the Los Angeles Times, Lear noted:

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.220
"People will tell you you can't do&nbsp;
just about anything — mention abortion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:53.400
have a gay character, write funny&nbsp;
about cancer. You do it anyway."

00:05:53.400 --> 00:06:00.420
"Christy Plunkett. Jill's sponsor.&nbsp;
But I'm not 'sponsible for this."

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.820
It was a big surprise to the viewers of "Mom" that&nbsp;
the character of Jodi, played by Emily Osment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:11.280
had a fatal overdose and died on the show. At that&nbsp;
point, Jodi had reached six months of sobriety.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.540
Lorre has said it was decided that Jodi would&nbsp;
have an overdose when she was first introduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:19.680
to the show. He explained the reasoning&nbsp;
behind this in an interview with TV Insider:

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:24.300
"Recovery from addiction is fragile. The&nbsp;
one day at a time trope is sadly true;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:28.440
it's a vulnerable state of being that&nbsp;
requires vigilance. You can't ignore it."

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:32.880
The entire cast of "Mom" was heartbroken when&nbsp;
they read the script and realized that Jodi was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:37.200
going to die on the show. Not only did they love&nbsp;
the character, but they loved Osment as well.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:41.220
It was a challenge for the writers of "Mom"&nbsp;
to figure out how to bring tragedy to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:45.960
show and then go back to being a comedy, but&nbsp;
ultimately, Jodi's death showed that life goes on.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:52.440
Laughter is contagious, and the shared experience&nbsp;
of laughing can make a comedy even funnier. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:56.520
is why it was so hard to shoot "Mom" when the&nbsp;
COVD-19 pandemic took away its live audience.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:00.600
"Something I said changed the&nbsp;
course of your life for the better."

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:03.360
"Feels like it."

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:04.620
"Are you listening to this?!?"

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:09.540
Veteran TV director James Widdoes, who helmed&nbsp;
episodes of "Mom," agreed with that assessment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:13.440
Speaking to Variety, he called shooting the&nbsp;
sitcom during the pandemic a "logistical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:17.940
challenge" like no other. Gemma Baker, the&nbsp;
co-creator of "Mom," told Deadline that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:21.780
not having a live audience was one of the&nbsp;
hardest parts of finally ending the series.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:25.980
"An audience was so much a part of what we&nbsp;
did and this whole season we missed them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:30.840
It really was a huge loss to not have that&nbsp;
energy and feedback and laughter. It would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:34.020
have been great if we [could've] ended&nbsp;
the season in front of a live audience."

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:39.600
When Anna Faris announced she was leaving&nbsp;
"Mom" after seven seasons, it was a big shock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:43.320
to say the least. And while it's hard for a&nbsp;
show to survive when a lead character leaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:48.300
it isn't unheard of. Similar to Chuck Lorre's&nbsp;
other hit sitcom, "Two and a Half Men," "Mom"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:51.720
was able to hang in there for a little&nbsp;
while longer, putting out one more season.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:56.460
However, co-creator Gemma Baker wishes they&nbsp;
could've done more with Christy, telling Deadline:

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:59.520
"There were so many things that we&nbsp;
wanted to address that we didn't get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.820
a chance to…if we had a little more&nbsp;
time we would have gotten a chance…"

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:06.660
One storyline fans missed out on was&nbsp;
seeing how Christy would've done at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:10.440
Georgetown University Law School.&nbsp;
Baker described how she imagined it:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:13.500
Christy would've faced some challenges&nbsp;
– but ultimately would've overcome them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:16.980
Baker went on to tell Deadline how&nbsp;
she envisioned closure for Christy:

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:19.560
"I like to think that [Christy] got into a happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:22.740
and healthy relationship and that she&nbsp;
is still a part of her mother's life."

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:24.720
Fans can have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:28.560
a lot of power when it comes to saving a&nbsp;
show. Case in point? The original "Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.880
Trek" series. About to be canceled after&nbsp;
the second season, NBC's deluge of letters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:37.560
from fans begging the network to keep it on&nbsp;
saved it. The cop drama "Cagney and Lacey"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:41.640
has a similar story – after it was canceled,&nbsp;
a letter writing campaign kept it on the air.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:46.680
And when "Mom" was officially canceled&nbsp;
in February 2021, fans mobilized with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:52.080
50,000 signature petition. And those fans who were&nbsp;
in recovery were particularly passionate. To them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:55.500
it was important for the show to remain&nbsp;
on the air, because of the fact that so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:58.920
many addicts were struggling during&nbsp;
the pandemic. As the petition stated:

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:04.800
"'Mom' has been a crucial voice in de-stigmatizing&nbsp;
addiction. This show has no doubt saved countless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:08.640
lives by promoting substance abuse recovery&nbsp;
and encouraging people to seek help."

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:11.220
While the show did end up going off the air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:15.540
the spirit of "Mom," and how it gave a&nbsp;
voice to people in recovery, will carry on.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:19.800
A lot of sitcoms have endings&nbsp;
where everything ends up just fine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:23.100
and everyone lives happily ever&nbsp;
after. But for a show like "Mom,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:26.760
having everything wrapped up with a neat&nbsp;
little bow at the end just wouldn't work.

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:30.720
"My name is Bonnie, and I'm a grateful alcoholic."

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:35.100
Co-creator Gemma Baker also didn't&nbsp;
want a convenient ending for the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:36.600
telling the Hollywood Reporter:

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:40.020
"Because of the nature of the show, it&nbsp;
didn't feel truthful to have an ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:43.200
where everything is tied up, because&nbsp;
the show has always really been about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:46.320
reality and characters showing&nbsp;
up for life on life's terms."

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.100
"I think it's true what we say here,&nbsp;
'don't leave before the miracle.'"

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:51.420
"Yuchh."

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:56.640
Baker recalled how the finale was hard to put&nbsp;
together because they wanted to bring the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:00.180
to a close in a way that was meaningful&nbsp;
and that did the best for the characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:04.440
and the audience as well, a challenging&nbsp;
feat even under the best of circumstances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:07.440
made more difficult by the&nbsp;
sudden departure of the lead.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:10.620
"We'll be there, every step of the way."
"Damn straight."

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:16.020
But it could also be seen as fitting, how, in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:19.800
end, the characters and the creative staff&nbsp;
had to make the best of a tough situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:22.500
and deal with life – on life's terms.

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:23.700
"FORGIVEN."

